      <p>If you need a 10 Gbit/s-ready VPS at $5/mo and want your traffic served from the US or Europe with IPv6 on by default, ServerHub is worth weighing against Vultr's $5 entry. If you want the absolute lowest entry price with built-in DDoS, RackNerd remains the reference. If you need 32+ regions and per-hour elasticity, Vultr is still the right pick. ServerHub is a <strong>10G-network mid-budget pick</strong>, not a universal replacement.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Who should choose ServerHub — and who shouldn't</h2>
      <p>ServerHub is a <strong>specific-value</strong> pick, and it is worth being explicit about the two sides:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-300">
        <li><strong>Choose ServerHub if</strong> you run a US- or EU-facing application that benefits from a 10 Gbit/s network port and IPv6-native stack — the 9-DC footprint plus Juniper + Cisco backbone is a strong fit for IPv6-first deployments, multi-region failover within the US + EU, and workloads that benefit from RAID-10 SSD storage with full root access.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose ServerHub if</strong> you need a long-running hosting brand with a verifiable operating history — the 2002 founding + 2× Inc 500 / Inc 5000 ranking is rare among newer budget hosts, and ServerHub has run the same network backbone (Juniper + Cisco + full BGP blended transit) for over a decade.</li>
        <li><strong>Choose ServerHub if</strong> you want bulk cache-backed storage without paying for the RAM you do not need — the SSD-Cached line scales to 7 TB of cache storage at $140/mo, useful for backup nodes or static-content hosts where the bottleneck is storage, not memory.</li>
        <li><strong>Avoid ServerHub if</strong> you need always-on in-line DDoS scrubbing at the VPS layer — RackNerd or Vultr include DDoS protection on their standard tiers, and ServerHub's VPS product pages do not advertise an equivalent.</li>
        <li><strong>Avoid ServerHub if</strong> you need an Asia-Pacific or South American POP, or 30+ global regions — ServerHub's 9-DC footprint is US + EU only, while Vultr and DigitalOcean offer much broader global reach.</li>
        <li><strong>Avoid ServerHub if</strong> you need per-hour billing or instant spin-up/down without a monthly commitment — ServerHub bills monthly without a public hourly option, so it is a poor fit for spiky or experimental workloads.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">Our Verdict</h2>
      <p>ServerHub earns a <strong>4.2/5</strong> for what it is: a 23-year-old US hosting brand with 9 verified datacenters, 10 Gbit/s-ready networking as standard, and a transparent Pure SSD + SSD-Cached pricing structure from $5/mo. The PRO I entry at <strong>$5/mo with 2 vCPU + 1 GB + 50 GB SSD + 1 TB transfer</strong> is competitive on spec for the price, and the higher tiers (PRO II through VALUE II) offer genuinely large SSD + RAM allocations for production workloads. The trade-offs — no built-in DDoS, mid-size 9-DC footprint, no hourly billing — make it a fit for buyers who value 10G networking, IPv6-native design, and a long operating track record over absolute lowest entry price or maximum region count.</p>
      <p>For cheapest-of-the-cheap unmanaged Linux with built-in DDoS, see our <a href="/vps/racknerd" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">RackNerd review</a>. For hourly-scaled global cloud with 32+ regions, see <a href="/vps/vultr" class="text-orange-400 hover:underline">Vultr</a>. For a 10G-network mid-budget host with a 23-year track record and a US+EU footprint, ServerHub is the strongest addition to the shortlist we have reviewed in 2026.</p>

